WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.203
President Biden

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.372
is running for reelection.

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:06.873
Everybody's talking about his age.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.908
People are talking about his polls.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.143
Even there's some challenges

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.578
within the Democrats.

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:12.946
Maybe somebody who might start to,

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.382
you know, to try to primary him, etc..

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.285
But what I would like to know is many say

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.820
that his policies

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.989
and his legislative and his wins,

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.591
frankly, should speak for themselves.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:25.959
And yet,

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.561
according to the way to win, it's a it's

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.330
a Democrat leaning company.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.200
Firm, only some 22% of Latino voters.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.704
33% of black voters can actually identify

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.706
something that they say he's done

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:42.242
to specific make their lives better.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:43.710
Right. What would you say to that?

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.911
And would you would

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:45.779
how would you advise him

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.881
to connect in a reelection?

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.682
I think

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:50.550
Joe Biden has done

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.484
an extraordinary job

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:52.185
leading the country

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:54.554
through some very difficult times.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:56.689
I do not think that there's going

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.158
to be any kind of serious

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.759
primary challenge to Joe Biden.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.528
I think the Democratic Party is unified.

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:05.298
You know, there was a lot of talk,

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.067
you'll remember when he was first elected

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:07.867
because Bernie

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:08.701
Sanders had run

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:09.369
that somehow

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:09.936
there was

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.204
this huge split

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:13.540
between progressive Democrats

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.841
and more centrist Democrats.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:17.143
And the truth is, is that partly

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.878
because of how Joe has governed

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.581
those divisions have been bridged.

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.950
I think what's true in American politics

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:24.684
generally is

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.119
until you get to campaigns,

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.188
people aren't paying much attention.

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.323
People have gone through a difficult time

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.858
because of COVID

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.561
and the pandemic and lockdowns

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.864
because of inflation,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.933
primarily the result of both

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.701
the war in Ukraine and

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.770
and rising energy prices,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.605
as well as supply chain issues.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:46.840
And so people have

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.808
memories about the eggs

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:49.142
got more

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:51.878
expensive and gas was more expensive.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:54.013
And they haven't been paying

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.381
as much attention

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.816
to the fact that, for example,

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.985
the African-American unemployment rate

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:02.222
is lower than it's been in decades.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.192
The campaign will allow

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.128
President Biden to make those arguments

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.997
and I think that, you know, in a

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.765
and in a media environment

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.167
that's so cluttered,

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.902
it's very hard to break through

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:17.537
until you get to election time.

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.505
You'll recall when I ran

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.874
for reelection in 2012,

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:23.042
my poll numbers weren't that great

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:26.880
and we ended up winning comfortably.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.114
Part of that was just

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.215
we started campaigning

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.783
and we were able to get a message out.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.953
People said, you know,

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.555
policy or this policy

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:36.756
or this thing left undone.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:37.924
That irritated me a little bit.

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:38.525
But

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.895
overall, I think he's done a good job,

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:42.862
and I think that's

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:44.130
what they're going to conclude

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.298
about Joe Biden as well.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.065
When Russia

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.734
started its illegal invasion,

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.836
the second invasion of Ukraine,

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.771
I believe you said that democracies,

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:52.972
it's a it's a clarion call.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:53.907
It's a wake up call.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.475
Democracies are getting flabby

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:56.543
and feckless.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.578
Where does Ukraine, in your view,

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:01.648
stand in the fight to preserve democracy?

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:02.515
I think it's vital.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:04.951
It's interesting.

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:08.087
Before I left office, I gave speeches

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.122
not just here in Athens,

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:12.025
but also in Hamburg and

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:12.659
London.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.128
And and and one of the arguments

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.262
I've made is

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.530
do not take for granted

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.098
the extraordinary achievement

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.767
of the European Union

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.335
and the fact that a continent

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.704
that was racked by war

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.141
and bloodshed for centuries

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:30.510
was now as prosperous

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.746
and as peaceful as any in history.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.282
And then now we've seen

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:40.053
the first war on European soil in

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.888
recent memory.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.157
And I think it was a wake up

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:44.924
call to Europe.

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.526
And I think it was a wake up

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:47.293
call to the West

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:49.762
and to democracies around the world that

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:54.167
the old ways of thinking might makes.

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:54.534
Right.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.768
Big countries can do

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.037
what they want to small countries that,

00:03:58.938 --> 00:03:59.606
you know,

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.242
people cannot independently determine

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.244
their futures

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:06.746
that that those forces have to be

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:07.880
confronted

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:10.950
Watching

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.820
the Ukrainians themselves

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.122
with such courage and bravery fight back,

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.324
I think that reminded

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.359
Europe of who they were

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:22.028
and I've been impressed by the degree

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:22.929
to which

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:26.032
and not easy circumstances.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.300
Europe has stood up.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.501
It has provided the aid

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.335
that was necessary

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.136
I think

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:32.105
the Biden administration has

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.140
very deftly managed

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.341
maintaining that alliance

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.210
to support Ukraine.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.177
And

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:41.814
and I believe the stakes are high

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.249
to send a message

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:46.286
to somebody like Putin that

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:49.322
they are not going to

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.592
just be able to willy nilly determine

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.561
the borders of other countries.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:56.129
So, obviously,

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.430
hindsight is a great thing.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.065
20, 20 vision is a great thing.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.300
But you experienced

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.200
while you were in office

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.368
Putin's first invasion,

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.003
the annexation of Crimea.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.071
And many people said

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.340
neither you nor the Western allies

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.574
stood up

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:09.742
and put enough red lines

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.210
around him around that.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.845
So what's your reaction to that?

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.747
And I want to just add also, your friend,

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:15.882
your good friend and colleague,

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:16.582
Angela Merkel,

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.484
is under very serious criticism

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:18.751
right now.

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.953
I know you've just met with her recently.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.889
Should she have leveraged,

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.691
you know, Germany's

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:27.393
economy, its energy on the addiction

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.262
to cheap energy in Russia?

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:30.263
Is that a mistake?

00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:30.897
Well, you know,

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:31.798
I think

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:34.834
the Ukraine of that time

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:35.868
is not the Ukraine

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:36.869
that we're talking about today.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:37.603
There's a reason

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:41.341
why there was not an armed invasion of

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:45.244
Crimea, because Crimea was

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:48.548
full of a lot of Russian speakers.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:50.016
And there was some sympathy

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:53.986
to the view that Russia was representing

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:54.987
its interests.

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.190
The Rada at the time,

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.192
the Ukrainian parliament itself

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.126
still had

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:02.128
a number of Russian sympathizers.

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:03.930
And the politics inside

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.032
Ukraine were more complicated.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.967
And part of what happened

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:11.270
was both myself, but also Merkel,

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:12.038
who I give

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:14.841
enormous credit for, had to pull in

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:17.343
a lot of other Europeans

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.711
kicking and screaming

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.280
to impose the sanctions that we did

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.550
and to prevent Putin from

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.052
continuing through the Donbas

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.054
into the rest of Ukraine.

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:31.557
So I actually think that given both

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:32.625
where Ukraine was at

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.092
at the time

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:33.693
and where

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:36.562
the European mindset was at the time,

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.231
we held the line.

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:41.734
And part of what happened was over time,

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:45.138
a sense of Ukrainian identity separate

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:48.408
from Russia and a determination

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:49.842
to push back against Russia

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:53.813
and an ability to prepare both militarily

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:57.450
and in civically to resist

00:06:58.017 --> 00:07:00.520
Russian pressure

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.688
that they built up those muscles.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:03.689
And that's part of the reason

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:04.290
why they were able

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.291
to respond the way they did.

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:07.293
When you actually saw

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.495
what was,

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:11.731
in my view, at least

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:14.500
incredibly misguided.

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:17.970
Not to mention illegal

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.273
and incredibly cruel incursion by air,

00:07:20.306 --> 00:07:21.240
by Russian forces.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:21.941
Do you think

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.009
Putin should have been challenged

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:23.876
more then?

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:25.878
I think that we challenged Putin

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:28.114
with the tools that we had at the time,

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.249
given where Ukraine was at the time.

